00:00:06.136 --> 00:00:08.800
MARCO CAVALLI: Good afternoon,
and welcome, everyone.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.200
As you know, hybrid
and multicloud

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.610
are big themes
this year at Next.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:19.570
Today, we want to put ourselves
in the shoes of a cloud

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.200
administrator to understand
the challenges of managing

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.980
large cloud workloads in
a context where hybrid

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.090
and multicloud are the norm.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.990
We will see how you can
leverage devops tooling and best

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.820
practices to manage your
cloud workloads consistently

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.240
across environments-- how
you can improve your security

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:46.560
posture, how you can establish
governance, and make compliance

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.190
easier at scale.

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.070
Yesterday we announced Anthos.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:55.730
And we showed our Anthos
conflict management

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.650
allows you to manage
your Kubernetes

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:02.630
environments on premise and
in the cloud through code.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.209
Today, we will walk you
through a live demo that

00:01:05.209 --> 00:01:09.800
shows how you can use Anthos
to manage your traditional GCP

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.120
resources and policies as code.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.460
My name is Marco Cavalli,
and I'm a product manager

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.840
in Google Cloud.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.170
And today, Alex and
Zhuzeng will join us.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.520
They are also from Google.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:26.120
And Alex will talk about
the config validator.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:27.880
And Zhuzeng will walk
us through the demo.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:33.050
So we said we want
to put ourselves

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:37.180
in the customer administrator or
the cloud administrator shoes.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.290
And I use the term
cloud administrator

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:44.410
broadly to refer to the most
common administrative functions

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.190
in Google Cloud.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:49.520
You can think of it as the
person or the team that

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.550
is responsible for security and
compliance for your company.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.340
Now the cloud administrator
is at the center

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.990
of a complex ecosystem.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.500
On one side, you have
requirements that your company

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.960
needs to meet.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.270
There are security,
compliance, governance,

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.740
auditability requirements.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.550
And these are top of mind
for pretty much all companies

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.140
across any industry.

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.620
And then you have stakeholders
that rely on the administrator

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.450
to do their job.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.270
There are developers
that need to move fast

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.270
but also want to know that
there are guardrails in place

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:30.170
to protect them.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.910
There are auditors that need to
have access to logs and change

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.960
history so that they can
sign off on your compliance

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.850
requirements with confidence.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.770
And there are
financial controllers

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.400
who want to be able to set
budgets for quarters and track

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.522
usage so that when the
cloud bill comes in,

00:02:49.522 --> 00:02:50.480
they are not surprised.

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:57.230
To support your stakeholders
and to meet your requirements

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.190
as the cloud administrator, you
have a large set of controls.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:05.390
You have authorization and
authentication policies

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.640
that control who can
use your resources

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.980
and how they can
use your resources

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.200
and which resources
they can use.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.240
You have organization
policies that

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.880
allow you to set
guardrails to make sure

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.960
that your users stay within
the compliance boundaries.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.670
You have firewall
rules that control

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.400
traffic between
different services

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:29.100
or different resources.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:33.470
All of these have different
interfaces, different best

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.640
practices, different syntaxes.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.330
These are knobs that you
use to control resources.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.030
And resources can
be in Google Cloud,

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.500
but can be also in
your data center.

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:47.230
Maybe you're running
traditional VMs

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:49.960
or you're running Kubernetes.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:52.540
Probably you have other
cloud providers too.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.700
So complexity can easily
become overwhelming,

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.750
especially if you're managing
everything manually, moving

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:03.460
from one UI to the other,
calling individual APIs.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.160
This is non-scalable
and error prone.

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:10.210
It also leads to
some challenges.

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.850
One of the challenges
is visibility.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:16.120
So how do you make
sure that you maintain

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:19.529
a comprehensive overview of
what's going on in your cloud

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:23.520
environments if you have to
deal with different interfaces

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.310
and different syntax is and
different best practices?

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:31.500
How do you ensure that loopholes
are not created somewhere

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:33.690
without you realizing?

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:36.900
Or how do you ensure that
your resources don't drift

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.360
after compliance over time?

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.670
And related to this
is auditability.

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:45.000
How do you keep track of changes
and how do you report them

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.310
in a way that is
easily consumable

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:51.300
by non-technical stakeholders?

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.710
And then as your business grows,
how do you make sure that you

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.780
don't become the bottleneck?

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.270
You said we want to enable
developers to move fast.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:02.910
So how do you do that
in a scalable way

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:07.270
to sustain a business growth
without slowing it down?

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.020
So again, doing things
manually is a problem.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:12.630
So standardization
and automation

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:17.000
are key to enable scalability.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.310
And finally, how do
you discover when

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.760
new features or new
configurations or policies

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:23.990
become available?

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.735
With cloud providers rolling out
new features at an increasing

00:05:27.735 --> 00:05:29.360
pace, how do you keep
up and how do you

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.960
ensure that your environments
are up to date all the time?

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.770
Well, you can go through
pages of documentations.

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:38.450
Or you can read blog posts.

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.260
Or you can reach out
to your cloud support.

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:41.870
But again, this is not scalable.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:46.370
How can we help you make it
simpler and more immediate?

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:49.790
And this, again, is in a world
where hybrid and multicloud are

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.150
just standard.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:56.900
So complexity is, in itself,
an issue and a challenge.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.010
But the more important
part is that complexity

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:05.850
may lead to an increased
vulnerability for your company.

00:06:05.850 --> 00:06:10.970
It is surprisingly easy to open
up a VM with sensitive data

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:15.650
through the internet, or to
give access to a storage bucket

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.470
[INAUDIBLE] data to
the entire world.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:21.433
These are examples
of misconfigurations.

00:06:21.433 --> 00:06:22.850
And we know that
misconfigurations

00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:26.960
are, in many cases, the root
cause of vulnerability or data

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:29.370
breaches and leaks.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:32.790
And misconfigurations
are, for the majority,

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:35.650
caused by human error.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:38.400
So how do we improve
your security?

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.990
And how do we reduce the
risk of misconfiguration?

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.830
Again, doing things manually,
pressing buttons in UIs

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:50.550
is dangerous, is not
easily trackable,

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.240
and is not controllable.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.560
So again, automation and
standardization are critical.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.390
So as I was learning about
this space a while ago,

00:07:01.390 --> 00:07:04.270
I was trying to understand
the separation of concern

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:07.150
between the administrator
and the developer.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:10.240
And my friend Ray came up
with an interesting analogy.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.490
So he told me, you
know when a company

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:16.030
moves to the cloud it's like
buying a large apartment

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:20.167
building with the goal of
renting out apartments.

00:07:20.167 --> 00:07:22.000
And I was like, oh,
that sounds interesting.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.920
But tell me more.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.240
And he said, think about it.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.940
The building has
an administrator.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:30.340
That's the building
administrator.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:34.510
And that is equivalent to
the cloud administrator.

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.150
And tenants or
prospective tenants

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:40.400
are like your
developers in the cloud.

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:42.140
So as the building
administrator,

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:45.280
you want to provide
apartments to your tenants

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:47.870
so that they can move
in and live there.

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:49.940
And in the cloud, as
the cloud administrator,

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:53.515
you want to provision resources
that are like the apartments.

00:07:53.515 --> 00:07:55.390
You want to provision
them to your developers

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:58.810
so that they can go on and
build their own application.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.650
And you want to do that within
the law in the apartment

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:07.540
building and within compliance
requirements in the cloud.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:12.430
So your goal is to
maximize your utilization,

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:17.750
free up your developers,
but stay within compliance.

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:20.070
And before you start
renting out apartments

00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:22.700
as the administrator,
you probably

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:27.140
want to set up some rules to
create a healthy environment.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.330
For example, you want
to set access rules

00:08:29.330 --> 00:08:33.919
and make sure that every tenant
has a key to enter the building

00:08:33.919 --> 00:08:36.510
or make sure that when
there are visitors,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:38.870
they check in at the front desk.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:41.960
And then you can use said rules
to control how the building

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:43.950
itself is used.

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:46.370
There are rules that, for
example, say there shouldn't

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:50.593
be parties after 10:00 PM,
or another rule that says,

00:08:50.593 --> 00:08:52.010
tenants shouldn't
set up a machine

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:55.460
shop in their apartment, or they
shouldn't be tearing down walls

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.450
like randomly.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:01.140
So these are like the basic
rules for a happy cohabitation.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.980
And in the cloud, the
equivalent of those rules

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.810
are the policies.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:09.740
You have policies that apply
to users, like authentication

00:09:09.740 --> 00:09:10.940
and authorization.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:13.580
You can enforce, for
example, that all your users

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:16.700
use a second factor
authentication to sign in.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.650
And when they are
in, you can control

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:22.350
which resources they
can access to do what.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.070
And then there are policies
that apply to resources.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.070
You may want, for example,
to organize resources

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:30.440
in a way that
creates separations

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:32.300
between the different tenants.

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:35.030
And you can do that
in GCP using folders.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:38.560
Or you can use the
namespaces in Kubernetes.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:41.270
And then you can use policies
like organization policies

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.740
to set guardrails in
compliance and governance.

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:47.780
Or you can use billing
and quota to control

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:50.240
usage, or networking
and monitoring,

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.990
and so on and so forth.

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:58.200
Now to wrap up this
analogy, before you rent out

00:09:58.200 --> 00:09:59.610
the apartments,
you probably want

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:03.660
to establish a process that is
easily scalable, repeatable,

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.000
and as automated as possible.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.980
And you can do that
by, for example,

00:10:08.980 --> 00:10:11.340
providing a few options.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:15.150
You can say that you have
a few sizes of apartments.

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:18.060
And they are like the
quota in the cloud.

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.730
Or you can have some
furnished apartment and some

00:10:20.730 --> 00:10:22.680
that are non-furnished.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:26.010
And then you can provide or
prepare different templates

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:27.580
for different contracts.

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:30.600
So when a new tenant
shows up, they

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:32.040
give you their requirements.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.900
You take the
corresponding contract.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.040
They sign it.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.690
And they are off to the races.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:38.190
And of course, you
want to make sure

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:42.660
that you control what's going
on so that things run smoothly

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:46.520
and nothing crazy happens.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.880
Now in the cloud, Anthos
Configuration Management

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:50.960
allows you to do that.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:54.800
It allows you to streamline
management of your environments

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:03.590
across cloud or on-premise
using code as the way to do it.

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:06.350
And it uses the Kubernetes
declarative style

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:09.170
to ensure that there is
a consistent experience

00:11:09.170 --> 00:11:13.120
across different environments.

00:11:13.120 --> 00:11:15.520
Because it uses code,
it stores that code

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:18.190
in a unified source
of truth, which

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:19.570
is stored in a GIT repository.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:23.530
And the GIT repository
is the user experience

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.930
around managing
your configurations

00:11:25.930 --> 00:11:29.420
and your infrastructure
across cyber environment.

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:32.110
And again, because
we use code, then you

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:35.050
can leverage things
like presubmit checks

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.030
to prevent bad
configuration from being

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:38.980
pushed into production.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:43.090
Or you can ensure that
whenever a change is submitted

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.580
and merged, it gets
automatically picked up

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.950
by the CI/CD pipeline
and automatically

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:53.065
deployed to the
corresponding infrastructure.

00:11:53.065 --> 00:11:54.690
Now let's look at
the config management

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.690
stack a little bit
in more detail.

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:00.420
There is an authoring
layer that is where

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:01.950
you define your configurations.

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:04.140
And we said we rely on code.

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.300
We use a GIT repository.

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:08.460
And we use the Kubernetes
declarative style.

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:12.340
And we'll look at that a
little bit in a moment.

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.570
The second key step
is the the validation.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:16.710
And again, it's a
way for you to define

00:12:16.710 --> 00:12:20.760
rules that prevent bad
configurations from being

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.040
pushed into production.

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:26.010
And this helps you in
two ways, primarily.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:29.940
One is that allows you
to establish governance.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:34.380
You can codify rules that
represents your requirement.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:37.050
And those rules need to
be met before anything

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:41.720
is pushed and merged
into the master branch.

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:45.050
And the second level
is it helps reduce

00:12:45.050 --> 00:12:48.780
toil for the administrator.

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.020
It does that because,
as the administrator,

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:54.740
you know that whenever a pull
request reaches your inbox,

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:57.320
it's already gone
through all your checks.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:59.990
So you can focus on the
real important things, what

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:02.360
needs to have human attention.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:04.640
You don't need to check
every single thing.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.710
And you could use
automation, in fact,

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:10.960
to limit the pull requests that
need to be reviewed manually.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.260
And I was talking to
a customer recently.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:15.560
And they have two
security administrators

00:13:15.560 --> 00:13:17.670
for the entire company.

00:13:17.670 --> 00:13:20.540
And for them, being
able to set those rules

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.400
and automate those
checks is critical.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:25.100
Because otherwise,
as today, they

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.010
have to review every
single pull request, which

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:31.010
is obviously non-scalable.

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.940
Now once the pull request has
been reviewed and submitted

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.580
and approved, then
it gets picked up.

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.090
It's merged into
the master branch.

00:13:38.090 --> 00:13:41.170
And is picked up
and actuated into

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:42.350
the corresponding platform.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:44.380
And this is where
things become real.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:46.390
On one side, you
have your intent.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:48.940
And the actuation layer
picks up your intent

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:52.030
and translates it
into API calls that

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:56.710
call the corresponding
settings and policy systems

00:13:56.710 --> 00:13:58.430
in the different platforms.

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:01.720
And finally, intelligence
gives you a recommendation.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:06.910
It uses machine learning to
look at your user pattern

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:10.510
to improve your
security posture.

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:14.170
Let's look at this layer
a little in more detail.

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:18.030
So we say that the authoring
layer is done through code.

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:19.670
And it's done in GIT.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:24.300
Now we are not prescriptive
as to where you should

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.500
host your GIT repository.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:31.520
But we are prescriptive on the
structure of the repository.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:35.070
The structure as
semantics and it

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:38.970
represents how your resources
are organized in the cloud.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:44.550
So we use folders to represent
projects and folders in GCP,

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:47.130
or namespaces in Kubernetes.

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:49.260
And files are YAML files.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:53.280
And they represent resources
and their policy bindings.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:56.610
And as we see in a moment, we
use the Kubernetes declarative

00:14:56.610 --> 00:15:02.430
style, again, to represent your
resources and your policies.

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:07.910
Because GIT is your source of
truth, then you can use easily

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.230
devops tooling and
best practices.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:13.660
You can build pre-submit checks,
like we said a moment ago.

00:15:13.660 --> 00:15:17.550
But you can also leverage things
like pull requests and approval

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:21.750
workflows so that changes
don't get merged automatically

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:22.890
into your master branch.

00:15:22.890 --> 00:15:25.470
But it has to be
reviewed and approved

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.960
by another administrator
before they become effective.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:31.770
And then, you can use
things like rollbacks

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:37.200
or gradual rollout so that
you don't immediately affect

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:40.590
all your users in one second.

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:43.740
Now, if you are an
existing customer and you

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:47.040
have a number of
resources already deployed

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:49.050
and you're managing
them manually,

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:53.280
you probably wonder,
how am I going to start.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.130
Well, we provide you
with an importer tool.

00:15:56.130 --> 00:15:58.020
And Zhuzeng will
show how that works.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:15:59.520
And this importer
takes everything

00:15:59.520 --> 00:16:03.150
you have in your
GCP environments

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:05.250
and dumps it into
a brand new repo.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.950
And you can then hit the
road running very quickly.

00:16:08.950 --> 00:16:11.970
And finally, because
everything is as code,

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:16.782
you can use templates.

00:16:16.782 --> 00:16:18.240
And templates are
like the contract

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.510
that we talked about
earlier for your developer.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:23.400
So you can build
your own templates.

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:26.190
But you can also access
templates from public library

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:27.510
or open source libraries.

00:16:27.510 --> 00:16:31.090
And Alex will talk
about that in a moment.

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:34.650
We are also working to
provide an extensive library

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:38.100
of templates with best
practices for different industry

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.310
verticals.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:42.750
There was a session yesterday
that talked about templates

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:44.670
for financial services.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:48.540
And over the course of
2019, we will extend those

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:52.580
to other industrial
verticals too.

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:58.160
So just a second on the
Kubernetes resource model.

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:00.420
It provides two benefits.

00:17:00.420 --> 00:17:04.069
One is that it is declarative
and it represents your desired

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:05.660
state.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:09.460
So the advantage here is
that you express your intent

00:17:09.460 --> 00:17:11.569
and you store it in
a GIT repository.

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:13.450
And then you can use
tooling to make sure

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:19.150
that your intent is deployed
and it is consistently refreshed

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:21.609
and mapped into your resources.

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:25.829
So that your resources
always reflect your intent.

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:28.140
And the second
benefit is that it

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:34.260
allows you to extract the
infrastructure using CRDs, so

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:35.940
Customer Resource Definitions.

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:37.620
You can create a
logical extraction

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:38.790
of your infrastructure.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:42.270
And you can use that to express
configurations and settings

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:45.390
in a consistent way across
different environments,

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:49.590
in a way that is infrastructure
agnostic so that it can easily

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:53.790
be mapped onto different
platform seamlessly.

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:58.390
Now the validation is
something that Alex

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:00.415
will talk about in more detail.

00:18:00.415 --> 00:18:01.540
So I won't spend much time.

00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:04.030
But just two quick notes.

00:18:04.030 --> 00:18:07.780
The validation, we said, helps
you prevent bad configuration

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:09.070
from being pushed.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:11.470
And you can do that by
defining your own rules.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.320
And those rules are like
two levels of controls.

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:16.240
One is like basic limiters.

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:19.240
You can use them to prevent
things like syntax errors.

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:21.250
Or if you are trying to
create a resource that's

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:23.690
already existent, it
will give you an error.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:25.480
Those errors are
caught immediately.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.330
So they don't even get
pushed into anything.

00:18:28.330 --> 00:18:31.770
They are caught and
stopped automatically.

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:34.000
And the second level
of control, you

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:38.410
can actually express rules
that represent your governance

00:18:38.410 --> 00:18:40.720
intent and your
governance requirements.

00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:44.510
So that whenever a
push goes through them,

00:18:44.510 --> 00:18:49.160
it only passes if it meets
your compliance requirements.

00:18:49.160 --> 00:18:51.980
We said the actuation is
where things become real.

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:54.800
So after somebody has
submitted a pull request,

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:56.990
that pull request
has been verified

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:58.970
through all the
automated checks,

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.190
and then has gone through
the administrator who

00:19:01.190 --> 00:19:04.280
has approved it, then it
is picked up automatically

00:19:04.280 --> 00:19:06.880
by the CI/CD
pipeline and deployed

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:09.350
to the corresponding
infrastructure.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:12.230
And in the demo, we will
focus on the actuation

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:17.020
for GCP resources specifically.

00:19:17.020 --> 00:19:19.540
Now, intelligence
is the last layer.

00:19:19.540 --> 00:19:22.300
And intelligence,
we said it helps you

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:25.630
optimize your security posture.

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:31.340
And you can do that
in different levels.

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:33.400
And we can use an analogy
from the car industry

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:34.850
to make it more clear.

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:39.040
So the first level is it helps
you understand what is going on

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:40.750
in your deployments.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:43.390
It helps you understand
which resources do you have

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:45.520
and what is the status
of those resources

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:48.710
and what the history
behind those resources.

00:19:48.710 --> 00:19:50.990
It's like the
dashboard in a car.

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:52.570
You have the speedometer.

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:53.770
You have the fuel gauge.

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:55.100
You have the temperature.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.640
But then decisions are
left to the driver.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:00.710
This is information that you can
use to make your own decision.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:04.130
But the tool is not
giving you more than that.

00:20:04.130 --> 00:20:06.610
But then the second layer
is actually the validator.

00:20:06.610 --> 00:20:10.630
And that helps you
prevent mistakes.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:14.740
It's like the blind spot sensor
in a car in the rear view

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:15.910
mirror of the car.

00:20:15.910 --> 00:20:19.020
So before you commit
to changing lane,

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.400
you make sure that
nobody is coming

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:24.040
so you can do that safely.

00:20:24.040 --> 00:20:26.560
The third layer is
the troubleshooter.

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:29.320
The troubleshooter
is Alpha for IM.

00:20:29.320 --> 00:20:32.470
And it helps you understand
when things are going wrong,

00:20:32.470 --> 00:20:34.490
why they are going wrong.

00:20:34.490 --> 00:20:37.360
For example, if I try
to spin up a new VM

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:39.470
and I get a
permission denied, it

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:41.080
helps you understand
which permissions

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:43.240
you need so that you
can do it correctly

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:47.410
or you can submit a pull request
to request those permissions.

00:20:47.410 --> 00:20:50.940
And it's like the diagnostic
system in the car.

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:54.830
It helps you understand
why the car is not working.

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:57.940
And finally, the more
intelligence part,

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:01.190
it uses machine learning
to analyze your usage.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:02.920
And it gives you
a recommendation

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:05.710
on how you can improve
your security posture.

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:08.770
And the IAM recommender
is also available today.

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:12.750
And the IAM recommender
looks at, let's say, Marco,

00:21:12.750 --> 00:21:15.640
he has 25 IAM rules.

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:18.400
But over the last six
months, he's only used two.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.218
So you may want to
reduce that, which

00:21:20.218 --> 00:21:22.510
brings you closer to the
principle of least [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:23.620
privilege.

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:28.080
And it generally reduces
your [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:32.640
And this is like maps in a car
that help you go somewhere.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:35.640
So let's bring it
all together now.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:38.490
We said we have a
source of truth.

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:40.590
It's in a GIT repository.

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:44.040
We are prescriptive about the
structure of the repository.

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:47.800
But you can host
it in any solution.

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:52.050
So we support GitHub or
GitLab or Cloud Source repo.

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:57.940
And everything is
stored as code.

00:21:57.940 --> 00:22:01.140
And then, you can plug
into any CI/CD pipeline.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:03.520
And then the validator
integrates with them.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:08.640
So in the demo, we will show
GitHub and Google Cloud Build,

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:13.980
but you can also use Travis, or
Jenkins, or Bamboo, and others.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:15.960
The actuation picks
up the changes

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:18.050
and deploys them
to the platform.

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:22.798
That can be GCP or it can
be Kubernetes on premise.

00:22:22.798 --> 00:22:24.090
And then the intelligence part.

00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:27.060
And the intelligence part
has the analysis part

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.460
that is for [INAUDIBLE]
is an open source tool.

00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.620
But you also have Cloud Asset
Inventory as I mentioned.

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:35.280
And then the intelligence that
gives you a recommendation.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:38.490
And those recommendations are
represented as pull requests.

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:40.890
And those pull requests
come from the tooling,

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:42.420
not from a human.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:45.070
And they are fed back
into the GIT repository.

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:46.440
So you can review them.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:49.230
And you can decide which
ones make sense to you,

00:22:49.230 --> 00:22:51.510
where do they impact
your resources,

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:55.460
and whether you want
to accept them or not.

00:22:55.460 --> 00:22:58.420
And with this, I would like
to invite Alex on stage.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:01.480
Alex will walk you
through the validator

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:02.710
and will introduce the demo.

00:23:05.422 --> 00:23:07.750
ALEX SUNG: Thank you, Marco.

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:09.530
Hello, ladies and gentlemen.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:12.730
So I'm Alex, a fellow
product manager working here

00:23:12.730 --> 00:23:14.860
on GCP and CSP.

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:16.360
As Marco mentioned,
earlier today we

00:23:16.360 --> 00:23:18.010
talked a lot about validator.

00:23:18.010 --> 00:23:20.680
I'm going to dive a
little bit deeper today.

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:24.190
At the same time, I'm also going
to be introducing our sample

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:26.800
customer over here,
Chili Oregano.

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:30.430
So Chili Oregano is
an e-commerce service

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.790
where with one tap you can
order the freshest, spiciest,

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:36.850
and most organic chilies
to your doorsteps.

00:23:36.850 --> 00:23:41.050
Now Chili Oregano recently
faced a data breach issue.

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:45.730
For some reason, somehow inside,
some of of their developers

00:23:45.730 --> 00:23:49.840
got gmail access into their
restricted and sensitive data.

00:23:49.840 --> 00:23:56.140
This essentially spun into
a breakout headline section

00:23:56.140 --> 00:23:58.540
where, hey, Chili
Oregano is not keeping

00:23:58.540 --> 00:24:00.460
their customers' data safe.

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:01.870
These things happen.

00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:04.270
They sound like
exaggerated situations.

00:24:04.270 --> 00:24:07.150
But they are real scenarios
that can happen to you.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:10.000
So we want to explore,
how can Chili Oregano stop

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:14.440
these type of incidences from
happening in the first place.

00:24:14.440 --> 00:24:16.560
Now let's introduce
some of the players

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:20.110
that we'll be talking about
very soon within our demo.

00:24:20.110 --> 00:24:22.530
The first is meet Alice.

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:25.320
Alice is our cloud
administrator.

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:27.215
And going back to
the building analogy

00:24:27.215 --> 00:24:29.730
that Marco mentioned
earlier, she

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:32.700
is similar to the
building administrator.

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.550
Alice cares deeply
about setting guardrails

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:38.610
around keeping the
environment safe and secure.

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.920
And at the same
time, though, Alice

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:44.070
does care about her developers,
one of their key stakeholders.

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:45.570
She wants to make
sure that they are

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:48.630
free to go build the best
applications and services

00:24:48.630 --> 00:24:50.100
possible.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:54.810
Today, Alice is managing all
of her resources manually.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:56.460
Tomorrow though,
Alice is looking

00:24:56.460 --> 00:24:58.980
to onboard into Anthos
Config Management

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:02.100
and be able to leverage
the powers of scalability

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:05.850
and automation.

00:25:05.850 --> 00:25:08.660
The second key player
we want to meet is Bob.

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:12.080
Now Bob is one of Chili
Oregano's top developers.

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:15.810
As I mentioned before, Bob
cares about moving fast.

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:17.750
He doesn't really
care about policies.

00:25:17.750 --> 00:25:20.870
He just wants to build cool
services and applications.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.410
But at the same time, Bob
knows about the dangers

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:26.150
of having insecure code.

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:28.640
He doesn't want to
be the perpetrator

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:31.760
of a deathly sort of headline.

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:34.380
So Bob cares about
his code being secure,

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:38.650
safe before it rolls
into production.

00:25:38.650 --> 00:25:40.510
And with that, let
me talk a little bit

00:25:40.510 --> 00:25:44.230
about validator, a solution
for a custom security

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:46.270
and governance guardrails.

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:51.100
How can validator help solve
both Alice the administrator

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:55.750
and Bob the developer's goals?

00:25:55.750 --> 00:25:59.530
Validator comes as a
two-pronged solution.

00:25:59.530 --> 00:26:03.280
The first side is during
pre-deployment checks.

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:06.760
How can we check whether
a configuration has

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:09.760
any violations before
we even provision it

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:12.970
or actuate it into
our platforms?

00:26:12.970 --> 00:26:16.180
In short, how do we stop
these misconfigurations

00:26:16.180 --> 00:26:18.380
before they happen?

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:21.580
The second is going to
be on ongoing monitoring.

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:24.720
And this acts sort of
as a checks and balance.

00:26:24.720 --> 00:26:28.440
If a violation does enter your
platform, how do we find it?

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.150
How do we identify it?

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:31.940
And here, when I talk
about violations,

00:26:31.940 --> 00:26:33.560
there are two key
ones that we have

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:35.820
to keep on top of our mind.

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:38.990
The first is break
glass violations.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:41.750
So as we mentioned, this
is an infrastructure

00:26:41.750 --> 00:26:42.680
as code solution.

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:44.570
Everything is
being done as code.

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:47.280
But developers, knowing
how smart they are,

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:50.430
sometimes they find things
around just to get things done.

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:53.390
What if a developer goes
directly into the cloud

00:26:53.390 --> 00:26:56.600
console, uses G Cloud,
or maybe even writes up

00:26:56.600 --> 00:26:58.970
their own script in order
to make some changes

00:26:58.970 --> 00:27:00.650
within your platform?

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:03.450
Now, for developers,
they may feel happy.

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:07.430
But as Alice the administrator,
we want to keep things secure.

00:27:07.430 --> 00:27:09.290
So with ongoing
monitoring, we're

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:11.300
able to identify
these violations

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:13.320
so that way Alice
the administrator can

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:15.480
decide what to do with them.

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:17.800
The second type of
violation that we care about

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:20.150
are retroactive violations.

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:23.900
So initially, your platform
has all of its resources

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:25.640
in a current state.

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:28.790
But as you employ new
constraints or new policies

00:27:28.790 --> 00:27:31.520
or new rules, some of
these old resources

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:33.410
may now be in violation.

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:36.710
How do we find and
identify these violations?

00:27:36.710 --> 00:27:39.200
So that way, Alice can
choose whether to remediate

00:27:39.200 --> 00:27:43.020
or to keep it as an exception.

00:27:43.020 --> 00:27:46.000
Now the glue that ties both
of these sides together

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.690
and the key to
validator is actually

00:27:48.690 --> 00:27:51.890
this single source
of truth, this repo

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:54.150
of validator constraints.

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:56.130
Imagine these constraints
as the ability

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:59.910
to write your governance, best
practice, and security rules

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.610
one time and have
it run everywhere

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:05.100
across tools, both in
the provisioning side

00:28:05.100 --> 00:28:07.140
and in the monitoring side.

00:28:07.140 --> 00:28:09.240
From audit
perspective, this also

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:11.940
makes it simple for you to
have one source of truth

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:14.790
for you to showcase, hey, here's
all of the actual governance

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:19.880
rules that are applied
within my company.

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.430
Diving a little bit
deeper, as Marco mentioned,

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:25.560
everything is going to be
defined as Kubernetes style

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:26.750
YAML.

00:28:26.750 --> 00:28:28.820
So Alice the administrator
can very simply

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:31.190
configure one of
these to instantiate

00:28:31.190 --> 00:28:33.598
a new policy or a new rule.

00:28:33.598 --> 00:28:35.390
Now there is a little
bit of business logic

00:28:35.390 --> 00:28:38.470
in the back end, which I'll
get to in a few slides.

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:42.500
But at the front,
very simply, if Alice

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:45.240
wants to create one of
these new constraints,

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:50.450
such as to stop the access
from a gmail account

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:54.120
or other outside domain,
how can she do that today?

00:28:54.120 --> 00:28:58.720
So here we have a sample with
domain restriction constraint.

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:01.630
And let me walk you through a
few of the different parameters

00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:03.180
we have here.

00:29:03.180 --> 00:29:06.150
Starting at the top, we
have the template kind.

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:07.890
So this defines
the type of check

00:29:07.890 --> 00:29:11.490
that is going to be done in
the background to enforce.

00:29:11.490 --> 00:29:13.230
And as I mentioned
already, this is

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:16.120
going to be the domain
restriction constraint.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:19.870
The second is a free form
text for the company's name.

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:23.290
So over here, Alice simply
names it a Chile Oregano domain

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:25.290
restriction.

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:27.390
The third is a piece
of metadata that's

00:29:27.390 --> 00:29:30.300
going to be very
critical, and no pun

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:33.300
intended, during your
remediation stages.

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:36.120
Alice wants to be able to
set whether a certain policy

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:37.770
or whether certain
constraint is going

00:29:37.770 --> 00:29:40.260
to be low, medium, or high.

00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:43.350
When this information is surface
to the ongoing monitoring

00:29:43.350 --> 00:29:46.200
tools, the tool
will then know, hey,

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:48.590
is this something
of low severity.

00:29:48.590 --> 00:29:50.400
So that way maybe we
should kick it off

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.490
to some sort of internal
workflow for ticketing

00:29:53.490 --> 00:29:54.540
approval.

00:29:54.540 --> 00:29:57.570
Or is it so severe,
such as giving access

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:00.690
to a gmail account on
our PII restrictive data

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:02.400
that we want to do
autoremediation?

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.680
We want to either remove
access immediately

00:30:04.680 --> 00:30:11.220
or we may even want to lock down
the resource in some other way.

00:30:11.220 --> 00:30:13.880
The fourth of these
is the target.

00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:15.830
What's the scope
of where you want

00:30:15.830 --> 00:30:17.970
this constraint to take place?

00:30:17.970 --> 00:30:21.200
So over here, based on
GCP's research hierarchy,

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:24.530
you could define it as the
organization, the folder,

00:30:24.530 --> 00:30:26.160
or the project level.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:28.440
In addition, you can
also set exceptions.

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:31.340
For example, it should only
apply to a set of projects

00:30:31.340 --> 00:30:34.260
while excluding some other ones.

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:37.278
And very lastly, we actually
get to the parameters.

00:30:37.278 --> 00:30:38.820
And this is what
Alice will care most

00:30:38.820 --> 00:30:41.640
about when she's configuring
one of these constraints.

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:44.700
These parameters will tie
directly back to the constraint

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:47.260
kind that we defined earlier.

00:30:47.260 --> 00:30:50.910
So in this scenario, since we
are limiting or restricting

00:30:50.910 --> 00:30:54.680
only a set of allowed
domains, Alice

00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:57.440
has set the gservice accounts,
so only Google service

00:30:57.440 --> 00:31:03.620
accounts is allowed, as well as
her own company Chili Oregano.

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:05.830
Now you may be wondering,
OK, we talked a little bit

00:31:05.830 --> 00:31:08.470
about these constraint
kinds and templates.

00:31:08.470 --> 00:31:10.510
Where do I actually get them?

00:31:10.510 --> 00:31:11.980
Well, this is
where Google, we're

00:31:11.980 --> 00:31:14.410
going with the open
source strategy.

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.980
The policy library is already
public and live today.

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:21.150
It is, again, open source,
community generated,

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:25.030
and it's going to be a single
location where we can imagine

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:28.340
different large players
across different industries--

00:31:28.340 --> 00:31:30.880
whether it's health
care, retail, finance--

00:31:30.880 --> 00:31:33.160
to be able to
contribute and build

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:35.860
different types of
constraints for their niche

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:39.880
or their industry
specific needs over time.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:42.210
At the same time
though, Google also

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:45.630
will be a key contributor
to this policy library.

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.060
We will be looking
at our best practices

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:51.780
from a security perspective,
product perspective, and even

00:31:51.780 --> 00:31:54.540
from our own professional
services teams.

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:57.030
These will be Google's
opinionated constraints,

00:31:57.030 --> 00:32:00.690
as we slowly push these
into the policy library.

00:32:00.690 --> 00:32:03.300
Starting today, though,
just for you to play with,

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:05.730
we will have a handful
of these constraints.

00:32:05.730 --> 00:32:08.910
I've already talked about
the IAM domain restriction.

00:32:08.910 --> 00:32:13.470
We will also have restriction
of VM instances with public IPs,

00:32:13.470 --> 00:32:16.560
enforcement of network
routing, and enforcement

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:17.370
of storage logging.

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:22.670
Now, I alluded to this
a little bit earlier.

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:25.040
For the simple
use case for Alice

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:28.130
where she just wants to use some
sort of preexisting constraint

00:32:28.130 --> 00:32:30.710
template, she just has to
instantiate the YAML file

00:32:30.710 --> 00:32:32.000
we showed earlier.

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:34.640
But what if Alice
wants to do more?

00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:36.560
What if she wants to
customize something?

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.980
What if she wants to have the
agility to constrain something,

00:32:39.980 --> 00:32:42.890
but Google or other players
within the community

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:45.930
has not provided inside
that policy library yet?

00:32:45.930 --> 00:32:50.090
Well, this is where we want
to empower the infrastructure

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:54.020
architects, cloud admin
teams [INAUDIBLE] to roll out

00:32:54.020 --> 00:32:57.170
and build their own custom
constraint templates.

00:32:57.170 --> 00:32:59.240
I won't go through the
exact details here.

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:01.940
But these templates
will be written in Rego.

00:33:01.940 --> 00:33:05.430
It is a dynamic and
popular policy language.

00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:08.990
And the example I have here
is for the actual enforcement

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:10.460
of storage logging.

00:33:10.460 --> 00:33:13.640
So as you can see, it's
only a small number of code

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:18.160
to do something that's
very, very critical.

00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:20.860
Circling back, let's
revisit what Alice and Bob

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:22.480
